---
import Feature from '../../components/Feature.astro'
import Snapshot from '../../components/Snapshot.astro'
import Specimen from '../../components/Specimen.astro'
import { FEATURES } from '../../features'
import FeaturePage from '../../layouts/FeaturePage.astro'
import type { PageMeta } from '../../meta'

export const meta = {
  description: FEATURES.tasks.sentence,
  sources: [
    'docs/workspaces-and-tasks.md',
    'docs/workspaces-and-tasks/projects.md',
    'docs/workspaces-and-tasks/task-creation.md',
    'docs/workspaces-and-tasks/worktrees.md',
    'docs/workspaces-and-tasks/project-config.md',
    'docs/workspaces-and-tasks/task-scripts.md',
    'docs/workspaces-and-tasks/archive.md',
    'docs/panes.md',
    'docs/panes/layout.md',
    'docs/tui.md',
    'docs/first-party-plugins/terminal.md',
  ],
  checked: '1d9bd200d',
} satisfies PageMeta
---

<FeaturePage feature="tasks" meta={meta} related={['agents', 'review', 'task-tools']} docs={{ href: '/docs/using/tasks-and-worktrees/', label: 'Read about tasks' }}>
  <Snapshot slot="picture" id="hero" />

  <Feature number={1} title="Workspaces, projects, and tasks" href="/docs/using/workspaces-and-projects/" link="Workspaces and projects">
    <p>
      A project is a folder on your machine, either a plain folder or a Git checkout. A workspace groups projects. A
      task is one piece of work on one project, and each task is a row in the rail.
    </p>
    <Specimen slot="picture" id="task-rows" />
  </Feature>

  <Feature number={2} title="A branch now, a worktree when it needs one" href="/docs/using/tasks-and-worktrees/" link="Tasks and worktrees">
    <p>
      <strong>New task</strong> makes a branch from the task's title, and adds <code>-2</code> if that name is taken.
      acorn creates the task's Git worktree the first time the editor, the Changes tab, a terminal, the preview, or an
      agent needs its files. You can start from another local branch, work in the project folder with no branch, or
      take over a worktree you already have.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <Feature number={3} title="A row of panes, kept for each task" href="/docs/using/panes/" link="Panes">
    <p>
      A task draws as a row of panes: the pull request, agents, Git, notes, the editor, and the rest. Pin, resize, and
      maximize them, and the task keeps its row. A pane with nothing to show offers no button, so a task gets no
      Workflows pane until it has run a workflow. A <code>[layout]</code> block in <code>.acorn/config.toml</code> can
      seed the row, start a run target in a terminal, and point the preview at it.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <Feature number={4} title="Setup and teardown scripts" href="/docs/using/task-scripts/" link="Task scripts">
    <p>
      The project's setup script runs in a new worktree as a terminal session called Setup, and the task's rail row
      shows a dot until it finishes. The teardown script runs when you archive the task. acorn keeps each run's result
      and the last 64 KiB of its output, so you can read what happened later, from the app or with{' '}
      <code>acorn task scripts logs</code>.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <Feature number={5} title="Archive cleans up, restore brings it back" href="/docs/using/archive-and-restore/" link="Archive and restore">
    <p>
      Archive asks each plugin what it has to say first, such as running containers, uncommitted files, or live
      sessions. Then acorn runs the teardown script, stops the task's terminals and agents, and removes the worktree.
      The task, its branch, its pull requests, its notes, and its agent sessions stay. Restore rebuilds the worktree
      and runs setup again, and an agent session picks up its conversation at your next message.
    </p>
    <Specimen slot="picture" id="ship" />
  </Feature>

  <Fragment slot="terminal">
    <p>
      The terminal client lists the same tasks in its <strong>Tasks</strong> panel, draws one pane at a time with a
      strip of pane labels above it, and shows setup and teardown status in the task's chrome. It has no terminal
      drawer and no Preview pane, so read a script's output with <code>acorn task scripts logs</code>.{' '}
      <a href="/docs/start/desktop-and-terminal/">Desktop and terminal</a>
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</FeaturePage>
